===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*selh₁-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|sellen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sellan||give; give up for money}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*salljan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*saljaną}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*selh₁-}}. Compare {{cog|da|sælge}}, {{cog|sv|sälja}}, {{cog|is|selja}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|sells|selling|sold}}

# {{senseid|en|peddle, vend, supply in exchange for money}} {{lb|en|transitive|ditransitive|intransitive}} To transfer [[good]]s or provide [[service]]s in [[exchange]] for [[money]].
#: {{syn|en|peddle|vend}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''sold''' her old car very quickly.}}
#: {{ux|en|I'll '''sell''' you three books for a hundred dollars.}}
#: {{ux|en|Sorry, I'm not prepared to '''sell'''.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Matthew|19|21|passage=If thou wilt be perfect, go and '''sell''' that thou hast, and give to the poor.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-08-10|volume=408|issue=8848|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21583270-new-zealands-plan-regulate-designer-drugs-better-trying-ban-them-and-failing-new A new prescription]
|passage=No sooner has a [synthetic] drug been blacklisted than chemists adjust their recipe and start churning out a subtly different one. These &ldquo;legal highs&rdquo; are '''sold''' for the few months it takes the authorities to identify and ban them, and then the cycle begins again.}}
# {{lb|en|ergative}} To be sold.
#: {{ux|en|This old stock will never '''sell'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|The corn '''sold''' for a good price.}}
# {{senseid|en|promote (a product or service) although without pay}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[promote]] (a product or service) although not being paid in any direct way or at all.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2016|chapter=The Fetal Kick Catalyst|title=w:The Big Bang Theory
|passage=Howard: You're gonna feel terrible when I'm in a wheelchair. Which, by the way, would fit easily in the back of this award-winning minivan.<br>Bernadette: Fine, we'll go to the E.R. Just stop '''selling''' me on the van.<br>Howard: You're right. It '''sells''' itself.}}
# {{senseid|en|promote (a viewpoint)}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[promote]] (a particular viewpoint).
#: {{ux|en|My boss is very old-fashioned and I'm having a lot of trouble '''selling''' the idea of working at home occasionally.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[betray]] for [[money]] or other things.
# {{lb|en|transitive|slang}} To [[trick]], [[cheat]], or [[manipulate]] someone.
#* {{RQ:Jonson Volpone|passage=Then weaues <br>Other crosse-plots <br>New tricks for safety, are sought; <br>They thriue: When, bold, <br>Each tempt's th'other againe, and all are '''sold'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1884|author=w:Mark Twain|title={{wsource|The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn}}|chapter=XXIII |passage=House was jammed again that night, and we '''sold''' this crowd the same way.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=January 12, 2011|author=Saj Chowdhury|work=BBC
|title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9345343.stm Blackpool 2-1 Liverpool]
|passage=Raul Meireles was the victim of the home side's hustling on this occasion giving the ball away to the impressive David Vaughan who slipped in Taylor-Fletcher. The striker '''sold''' Daniel Agger with the best dummy of the night before placing his shot past keeper Pepe Reina.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|professional wrestling|slang}} To pretend that an opponent's blows or maneuvers are causing legitimate injury; to act.
# {{lb|en|AU|slang|intransitive}} To [[throw under the bus]]; to [[let down]] one's own [[team]] in an [[endeavour]], especially in a [[sport]] or a [[game]].
#: {{ux|en|He's '''selling'''!}}
#: {{ux|en|He really '''sold''' in that match.}}

